C++ version

A write-once-read-many (WORM) synchronization lock allows multiple threads to read concurrently, but permits only a single write operation ever, after which writing is permanently disabled or blocked.Conceptual DesignState tracking: Keep a counter or flag for the write status (e.g., written = false, active reader count).Reader lock: Allows multiple concurrent readers as long as a write is not actively happening, incrementing a reader count.Writer lock: Allowed only if no readers are active and no previous write has occurred. Once the write completes, it sets the written flag to prevent future writes.C++ Implementation ExampleYou can implement this using standard components like std::mutex and condition variables, or standard shared locks. Here is a clean pattern:cpp#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class WormMutex {
private:
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    int reader_count = 0;
    bool is_writing = false;
    bool has_written = false; // Ensures write-once constraint

public:
    void lock_read() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        // Wait while a thread is currently writing
        cv.wait(lock, [this]() { return !is_writing; });
        reader_count++;
    }

    void unlock_read() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        reader_count--;
        if (reader_count == 0) {
            cv.notify_all();
        }
    }

    bool lock_write() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        // Fail or block if already written once or currently reading/writing
        if (has_written || reader_count > 0 || is_writing) {
            return false; 
        }
        is_writing = true;
        return true;
    }

    void unlock_write() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        is_writing = false;
        has_written = true; // Mark as permanently written
        cv.notify_all();
    }
};


C version
A Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) synchronization structure in C allows a single writer to set a value once, after which an unlimited number of reader threads can access it concurrently without locking overhead.Here is a clean implementation using POSIX threads (pthreads) and atomic operations (stdatomic.h) for state management:WORM Mutex Implementation in Cc#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <unistd.h>

typedef enum {
    WORM_EMPTY = 0,
    WORM_WRITING,
    WORM_READY
} worm_state_t;

typedef struct {
    atomic_int state;       // Tracks lifecycle: EMPTY -> WRITING -> READY
    pthread_mutex_t lock;   // Ensures only one writer attempts initialization
    int data;               // The shared payload
} worm_t;

// Initialize the WORM structure
void worm_init(worm_t *w) {
    atomic_init(&w->state, WORM_EMPTY);
    pthread_mutex_init(&w->lock, NULL);
    w->data = 0;
}

// Writer tries to write once. Returns 1 on success, 0 if already written/writing.
int worm_write(worm_t *w, int value) {
    // Fast path: check without locking if already written
    if (atomic_load_explicit(&w->state, memory_order_acquire) == WORM_READY) {
        return 0; 
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&w->lock);
    
    // Double-check state under lock
    if (w->state != WORM_EMPTY) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&w->lock);
        return 0;
    }

    // Mark as writing
    atomic_store_explicit(&w->state, WORM_WRITING, memory_order_relaxed);
    
    // Perform the single write
    w->data = value;
    
    // Publish the data and transition to READY with release semantics
    atomic_store_explicit(&w->state, WORM_READY, memory_order_release);
    
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&w->lock);
    return 1;
}

// Reader reads data safely without locking once it is ready
int worm_read(worm_t *w, int *out_value) {
    // If data is not ready yet, read fails
    if (atomic_load_explicit(&w->state, memory_order_acquire) != WORM_READY) {
        return 0;
    }
    
    *out_value = w->data;
    return 1;
}

// Cleanup resources
void worm_destroy(worm_t *w) {
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&w->lock);
}
Use code with caution.How It WorksLock-free Reads: Once state transitions to WORM_READY, reader threads call worm_read which only performs an atomic load with acquire memory ordering—completely bypassing mutex contention.Exclusive Single Write: The standard pthread_mutex_t is used exclusively during the write phase to prevent multiple concurrent writers from racing to populate the value.
